        <item>
            <title>3.12.0</title>
            <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 14:02:24 +0900</pubDate>
            <link>https://github.com/Saqoosha/Canopy</link>
            <sparkle:version>177</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>3.12.0</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>15.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <sparkle:hardwareRequirements>arm64</sparkle:hardwareRequirements>
            <description sparkle:format="markdown"><![CDATA[### Features
- **Keep the Mac awake with the lid closed.** While a session is busy, Canopy now also keeps a MacBook awake with its lid closed, on battery and without an external display. Plugging in with the lid closed no longer puts it to sleep either.
- **Stay reachable remotely** (Settings → General, on by default): keeps the Mac awake even with nothing running, so the phone or another Mac can reach it at any time.
- **Battery threshold slider**: choose the battery level (5–95 %, default 20 %) below which the Mac is allowed to sleep anyway.
- **Awake indicator**: a cup icon in the sidebar footer while Canopy is keeping the Mac awake; hover it to see why.
- **Battery notices on your phone**: while a lid-closed Mac is held awake on battery, Canopy Mobile is notified when that starts, at every 10 % drop, and when the Mac is about to sleep.
- Control API: `listen` waits for session events (turns finishing, permission requests, questions, sessions opening and closing), and `history` returns a session's recent turns.

### Changes
- The "sessions on an older extension" popover has a single **Restart All** button instead of one per session, and warns before restarting busy sessions.

### Bug Fixes
- The extension's rate-limit warning banner no longer appears in every pane.
- Panes on another Mac re-attach immediately when this Mac restarts a session in place, instead of showing a disconnected state.
- The sidebar line about sessions on an older extension no longer gets cut off.

### Notes
- Use display sleep rather than the monitor's power button: powering off a connected display with the lid closed still lets the Mac sleep.
- Unplugging power with the lid closed still lets the Mac sleep.
]]></description>
